@vsm 1.0
// docs/incidents/2026-08-03_ds_dispatch_chain_reconstruction.vsm
// DIRECTIVE: [O]@S3 -> [S]@S1 (DS), 2026-08-03
// Requested by rmw3, verbatim intent (2 turns): "pidamosle ayuda a DS para
// que nos ayude a desenredar la cadena de acontecimientos del incidente y
// que nos reporte un canal de accion, que nos ayude a recordar todos los
// elementos que tenemos que considerar para esta mejora" -- then, after
// Claude found a live boot-memory doc bug mid-prep: "si, agregalo... quiero
// que revise todo lo que hicimos en las ultimas 48 horas, incluido esto."
// SCOPE WIDENED accordingly: this is now an independent AUDIT of the last
// 48h of work (2026-08-01 through 2026-08-03), not only the NATS/clima/
// cgroup incident cluster -- DS should not simply trust Claude's curated
// reading list below, it is a floor, not a ceiling.

phi([O]@S3 -> [S]@S1, directive) {
  ref:  docs/incidents/2026-08-03_ds_dispatch_chain_reconstruction.vsm
  auth: rmw3 (S5), 2026-08-03, live session -- asked directly for DS's help on this
  why:  "Two sessions (2026-08-02/03) surfaced a lot of connected material --
      a NATS token leak/crash-loop incident, a separate finding that
      s1-clima starved LXC 117's disk I/O for 12+ hours undetected, a
      confirmed cgroup enforcement gap (cpu.max/memory.max/io.max all
      effectively unlimited), AND (found mid-prep for THIS dispatch) 6 dead
      doc links in CLAUDE.md itself (agent boot memory) plus 4 more stale
      cross-references inside the moved docs themselves -- fixed live by
      Claude, see thread (e) below -- documented/scattered across 5+
      separate .vsm/.md files with overlapping but not identical scope.
      rmw3 wants the CHAIN untangled (is this one causal story or several
      tangled together?), ONE consolidated action plan + checklist, AND an
      independent second pass over the full 48h window in case anything
      else got missed -- the doc-link bug is itself proof that a single
      pass (README.md's fix earlier today) can look complete and not be.
      Same R4 discipline this whole incident has run on: a claim of
      completeness is not evidence of completeness."

  scope_and_guardrail_critico: "READ-ONLY investigation + writing. NO infra
      changes, NO service restarts, NO LXC start/stop/create/destroy, NO
      config edits, NO deploys, NO NATS/token touches. This produces a
      REPORT for rmw3's review, not executed action. Standing gate still
      applies and is NOT being crossed by this dispatch:
      S1-SOC-REOPEN-GATED-ON-S2S5-REVIEW (current_focus.json) -- rmw3,
      verbatim: 'aun no vamos a encender los s1 ni socs... vamos a probar
      y repasar todo el sistema s2-s5 primero.' If anything found while
      reading tempts a live check ('let me just restart X to confirm'),
      STOP and note it as a RECOMMENDATION in the report instead of
      executing it.",

  task: {
    source_material_read_first: [
      "docs/incidents/2026-08-02_clima_nats_observability_incident.vsm",
      "docs/incidents/2026-08-02_recovery_plan.vsm",
      "docs/spec/thermal_resource_governance/amendment_2026-08-02.vsm",
      "docs/spec/sensor_omega_integration/spec_2026-08-02.vsm",
      "docs/spec/nats_token_registry.vsm",
      "TASKS.vsm -- search these exact ref strings: THERMAL-RESOURCE-
          GOVERNANCE-AMENDMENT, THERMAL-WATCHDOG-MISSING,
          ALGEDONIC-STORAGE-BLINDSPOT (already CLOSED 2026-07-26 -- read
          for the pattern/precedent, do not re-open or redo it),
          S3-ALLOC-NATS-CRASHLOOP-UNROOTED, NATS-TOKEN-ROTATION-2026-08-03",
      "current_focus.json -- ALL entries, not just the incident-named ones
          (the full array is short; read it whole)",
      "HANDOFF.vsm.md -- ALL session(...) blocks from the tail of the file
          back through session(\"2026-08-02...\") (several sessions, not
          just the two named above -- this is the floor, not the ceiling)",
      "git log --oneline --since='2026-08-01 00:00' --until='2026-08-04 00:00'
          -- run this yourself, don't rely on Claude's memory of it. Skim
          every commit touching docs/, TASKS.vsm, CLAUDE.md, RULES.md,
          scripts/ for anything relevant that isn't already named here.",
      "THIS FILE's own diff -- git log -p -1 -- CLAUDE.md docs/guides/
          concepts.md docs/guides/human_guide.md docs/theory/
          recursive_vsm_theory.md (Claude's live fix, same session, for
          thread (e) below -- read the actual diff, don't take the commit
          message's word for completeness).",
    ],

    deliverable_0_independent_audit: "Before writing anything: do your OWN
        pass over the 48h window (git log + HANDOFF.vsm.md session blocks,
        per source_material_read_first above) independent of the thread
        list (a)-(e) named in deliverable_1 below. rmw3's explicit ask:
        review EVERYTHING done in the last 48 hours, not just what Claude
        already flagged -- the doc-link bug is itself the proof that a
        single pass can look complete and not be. If you find something
        that fits the same pattern (a fix/finding that looks closed but
        has an unexamined edge, a claim not independently verified, a doc
        that drifted from what it describes) ADD it as a thread (f), (g),
        ... with the same treatment as (a)-(e). If you find nothing beyond
        (a)-(e), say so explicitly in the report -- 'independent pass
        found no additional threads' is a real, useful result, not a
        non-answer.",

    deliverable_1_reconstruction: "ONE chronological timeline (not a list of
        separate per-doc summaries) across: (a) the NATS token leak /
        rotation / crash-loops, (b) s1-clima's ~38x I/O overconsumption
        starving LXC 117 (2026-08-02 morning case study), (c) the missing
        cgroup limits (cpu.max/memory.max/io.max effectively unlimited on
        101/111/107), (d) the disk-contention symptoms (wazuh-db D-state
        on 116, dpkg stall on 117, s3-alloc's CURRENT unrooted NATS
        crash-loop, 1104 restarts), (e) the doc cross-reference staleness
        found today (README.md's fix earlier in the session only patched
        its own tree-diagram copy; CLAUDE.md's 6 dead links and 4 stale
        internal @ref lines inside the moved docs themselves were both
        still broken until Claude fixed them live during this same
        session -- ALREADY CLOSED, include as a closed thread, not an
        open one). For each event: date, what actually happened (cite the
        source doc + line/section), and whether it is a CAUSE, SYMPTOM, or
        INDEPENDENT event relative to the others. State explicitly where
        the causal chain is UNCERTAIN rather than guessing -- e.g. LXC
        107's cpulimit:1-vs-unlimited-cgroup discrepancy is flagged
        unexplained in the source docs; leave it flagged, don't resolve it
        by assumption. Thread (e) is structurally different from (a)-(d)
        (a doc-hygiene bug, not an infra incident) -- say explicitly why
        rmw3 grouped it in anyway: same root pattern (a fix that LOOKED
        complete but only covered the copy someone happened to be looking
        at, not the underlying thing) as (a)-(d)'s repeated near-misses
        (2 missed token locations on the first NATS sweep, io.weight
        silently doing nothing under the wrong scheduler, etc).",

    deliverable_2_action_plan: "ONE sequenced plan reconciling
        recovery_plan.vsm's phases B/C/D with
        thermal_resource_governance/amendment_2026-08-02.vsm's 4 threads --
        these are two separate documents describing overlapping work.
        State explicitly where they overlap and where they're distinct.
        thermal_resource_governance's own doc flags thread 4 (cgroup/IO)
        as 'the most mechanically independent, could be picked up on its
        own schedule' -- evaluate whether that still holds given
        everything else now queued (S3-ALLOC-NATS-CRASHLOOP-UNROOTED in
        particular touches the SAME subsystem, s3_resource_alloc.py on
        LXC 101), or whether a dependency exists that doc didn't have
        visibility into when it was written.",

    deliverable_3_checklist: "A flat, exhaustive checklist -- one line per
        distinct technical element found across the source docs, each
        with its current status (done / open / blocked) and source doc
        reference, scannable in under a minute. Minimum set to include:
        cgroup cpu/memory/io limits, PSI io.pressure consumers (zero
        today), io.weight's BFQ-scheduler dependency (n02 defaults to
        mq-deadline), Component B (S4-anticipation host cgroup executor)
        deployment status, the SSD migration's relationship to the disk-
        contention thread, the generic liveness scanner (recovery_plan
        phase B1), config centralization (phase C), the certification
        battery (phase D) and its still-undefined pass/fail threshold,
        the doc cross-reference staleness (thread (e), CLOSED 2026-08-03
        -- list it as done, but note phase C's config-centralization
        principle is the durable fix, since nothing today prevents this
        exact bug recurring the next time a doc moves), and any thread(s)
        (f)/(g)/... surfaced by deliverable_0's independent audit pass.
        Add anything else found in the source docs that fits this shape.",
  }

  sequencing_override: "PAUSE dispatch A in full (the pre-approved
      s3_resource_alloc.py bundle: DYN-LLM-GRANT P2+P3, TIER-2 of
      dynamic_resources_all_lxc.spec.vsm, the S3-ALLOC-NATS-CRASHLOOP-
      UNROOTED diagnosis/fix, A0-ALLOC-BASELINE-SYNC check) until this
      reconstruction report is delivered and reviewed by rmw3 + Claude.
      rmw3's explicit decision, 2026-08-03: dispatch A touches the SAME
      file (s3_resource_alloc.py) this report's action plan will make
      recommendations about -- diagnosing or fixing the crash-loop before
      the cgroup/IO picture is clear risks touching that file twice
      without knowing whether the fix should be sequenced differently.
      Dispatches C and D (S4-MULTI-S1 Phase 2 + EXP-LL-01-P1;
      AUTOIMMUNE-TEST) are UNAFFECTED -- they don't touch
      s3_resource_alloc.py and may proceed on DS's own schedule if DS
      has bandwidth after this report.",

  gate: "Design/report only -- wait for rmw3 + Claude review before any of
      this becomes a dispatched implementation task.",

  result: "Write the report to
      docs/incidents/2026-08-03_incident_chain_reconstruction.vsm (VSL,
      per R15 -- formal cross-agent output, never prose/.md). Commit on
      feat/ds-s1 with a clear commit message referencing this dispatch
      file. Do NOT edit TASKS.vsm or HANDOFF.vsm.md directly -- Claude
      folds the result in during review, per the standing merge-gate
      convention ([[project_ds_agent]]).",

  after: "Reply in VSL (phi([S]@S1 -> [O]@S3, report) { ... }) once
      committed, per R15 -- not a plain-prose tmux message.",
}
